JavaScript is primarily considered an interpreted language because it doesn't require a separate compilation step before execution. However, modern JavaScript engines, such as V8 (used in Chrome and Node.js) and SpiderMonkey (used in Firefox), utilize Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation.

Key Points:
Interpreted Language: Traditionally, JavaScript is executed directly by an interpreter in the browser. This means the code is read and executed line by line without a prior compilation step.
JIT Compilation: To optimize performance, modern JavaScript engines compile code into executable bytecode at runtime, just before it is executed. This hybrid approach allows JavaScript to have both interpreted and compiled characteristics.

In the context of JavaScript's Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation:

The JavaScript code is first converted into executable bytecode.
This bytecode is then executed by the JavaScript engine, which acts as the interpreter.
